  "response": "I'd strengthen **`crime_00042`** first — the only weak-test finding whose name explicitly targets an esbuild plugin.\n\n**Evidence (quoted, not paraphrased):**\n- `\"test: \\\"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\\\"\"`\n- `\"0 expect/assert calls\"`\n- `\"lines 537-548\"`\n- file: `test/index.test.ts`\n- summary: `\"A test that only runs setup gives agents false confidence.\"`\n\n**Why this one over the other 11 weak-test findings:**\n\nThe reported regression is \"an esbuild plugin regression slipped through CI even though the relevant test still passes.\" That description matches a test that *runs* the plugin path but never asserts on its output — exactly what `crime_00042` flags. The other 11 zero-assertion tests target dts/tsconfig/glob/shim behavior, not an esbuild plugin. `crime_00043` (`removeNodeProtocol works on shims`) is the closest runner-up but operates on shim string transformation rather than a plugin pipeline.\n\n**Specific assertion I'd add:**\n\nThe test name promises that the `native-node-module` plugin handles `*.node` and `*.node.js` imports \"properly.\" A passing test with zero `expect` calls only proves the build didn't throw. The assertion that closes the regression gap is on the **emitted bundle's import form** — read the output file the build produced and assert that:\n\n```ts\nconst output = await readFile(outFile, 'utf8')\nexpect(output).toMatch(/require\\([\"'][^\"']+\\.node[\"']\\)/)\nexpect(output).not.toMatch(/from [\"'][^\"']+\\.node(\\.js)?[\"']/)\n```\n\nThat pins the actual contract the plugin owns: `.node` / `.node.js` imports get rewritten to a runtime `require(...)` of the native binding rather than left as an ESM import esbuild can't resolve. Without that assertion, the plugin can silently regress to passing the import through unmodified and the test still goes green — which is the failure mode described.",
